@arc raman
who: raman
// Stage 2 (ST1, 2026-10-31): baked from here (content/stories/stage2/, read only while STORIES2_ON); the words are
// content/stories/waiting/raman.story's, unchanged but for one speaker (beat 5); the notes became fields.
// "The same comet" (content/arcs/outlines.md, Crew stories I). SW3, 2026-10-24. Soft and exact; careful, proud,
// gentle; talks about flying the way some people talk about carrying soup. needs: listen. New canon (the outline): the
// near miss at Lisk years ago (a cargo arm swung wrong, nobody hurt, his small daughter at the dock gallery glass); his
// elder daughter Asel, seventeen, wants to fly. Never the desk job (not this story), never the comet knot or Saruth,
// never First Light Cocoa retold. Sol Bakshi (they / them) is a helm rating on nights, from Lisk too.
// Numbering: beat 5 is the crescendo; beat 6 the quiet ending for beat 5 (it gives the same keepsake).

== beat 1
tone: light
tier: acquaintance
where: Observation room
when: time:morning
hint: Ah, hello. Early, aren't you? Sit. The view's slow at this hour.
mem: soup
them: Ah, hello. Early riser too? This is the finest window aboard before seven. Don't tell the Captain.
them: People ask me the secret of flying. It's soup. Carry a full bowl across a room. That's all it is.
them: You don't look at the bowl. That's where they go wrong. You look where you're going, and you go gently.   [gesture]
them: I flew you in, you know. The Tern, your first night. You gripped the seat strap the whole way down.
them: Everyone does. I like that. It means they trust the strap more than the pilot. Very sensible.   [laugh]
? choice
* Was it a good landing?   flavour:listen mem:landing
  them: Smooth. I was pleased with it. I'm pleased with most of them, quietly.
  + them: Very smooth. I logged it. I log the good ones in my head, the way some people keep a diary. Thank you for asking.   [nod]
* I'll trust the pilot next time. Probably.   flavour:cheer mem:probably
  them: Probably is plenty. Hold the strap anyway.   [laugh]
* I carry my tea like that. Eyes front.   flavour:share mem:tea
  them: Then you're halfway to the helm already. Gently, now.
them: There. The sun's catching the hull on the port side. Every morning. I never tire of it.

== beat 2
tone: light
where: Observation room
when: beat:raman.1
hint: Have you a minute? I've a small puzzle, and it's the size of my thumb.
mem: model
them: My model of the Wren. You've seen it by my door. Every hull plate, every window. She's finished.
them: Except her shuttle bay. It's empty. No shuttle in it. She looks as if she's lost something.
them: I've carved three. Three little shuttles. Threw every one away. Wrong nose. Wrong wings. Wrong.   [sigh]
them: And the docking arm in the bay keeps drooping. It wants a pin. A brass one, very small. I've no lathe.
? choice
* Why does this one matter so much?   flavour:listen mem:whymatter
  them: I'm not sure yet. I'll tell you when I am.
  + them: I'm not sure yet. Some things you make carefully because you can't say them. I'll tell you when I can.   [nod]
* Three shuttles in the bin. The bin must be very proud.   flavour:cheer mem:bin
  them: The bin has a better fleet than the Survey Service.   [laugh]
* I could make you the pin in the machine shop.   flavour:practical favour:ramanPin flag:raman.pin mem:pin
  them: Would you? Oh, that's lovely. Thinner than a match, and polished. Thank you kindly.
them: I'll try a fourth shuttle. Slowly. The fourth one's usually the one.

== beat 3
// As built (ST1): where: calm (he's in the observation room only about one evening in three; nothing here names it)
tone: heartfelt
where: calm
when: beat:raman.2 time:evening
hint: Sit with me a while? Something I don't say much. I'd like to, to you.
mem: lisk
them: Twenty years of clean flying. People say that about me. I say it too. It's nearly true.
them: Years ago, docking at Lisk, a cargo arm on the dock swung wrong. Too close to us. I pulled us off it.
them: Nobody was hurt. Not a scratch on anyone. But Asel was at the gallery glass, waving. She was six.   [sigh]
them: She saw her dad's ship nearly touch that arm. She went very quiet. So did I.
them: Since then I fly every hard part myself. Every docking, every long turn. Nobody else's hands.
them: Now Asel's seventeen. She wants to learn to fly. She asked me. I couldn't say the word.
? choice
* You've carried that a long time. Go on.   flavour:listen mem:carried
  them: Eleven years. It's lighter than it was. It's just never quite put down.
  + them: Eleven years. Saying it to you, I can hear how long that is. Thank you for letting me be slow about it.   [nod]
* Six-year-olds wave at everything. She was probably waving at the arm.   flavour:cheer mem:waving
  them: Ha. She might have been. She liked big machines. Still does. That's the trouble.   [laugh]
* I had a scare once that I never told anyone about.   flavour:share mem:scare
  them: Then we each know one. That's a fair trade.
them: She has my hands, you know. Steady. I've watched them. Ah, me. That's the trouble too.

== beat 4
// DAY: 8-21
// friend, 2+ days after beat 3, before the comet (Days 8-21: the comet moved to Monday Day 22, FX25 / EP1's events-plan-2 A5)
// As built (ST1): where: calm, as beat 3 (it must come by Day 21)
tone: heartfelt
where: calm
when: beat:raman.3 !event:comet day:8-21
hint: May I ask you something? It's a question for you, not about flying. Well. A bit.
mem: trust
them: Comet nights, the helm brings the bow round so the comet crosses our big window. Slow, very slow.
them: I've flown that turn myself every time. Every comet for twenty years. It's my favourite thing I do.
them: This year I'm thinking of giving it to Sol Bakshi. Eight months aboard. Good hands. From Lisk, like me.
them: Bakshi doesn't know yet. I keep nearly telling them, and then I straighten the charts instead.   [laugh]
them: So I'll ask you. Would you trust someone new with something you love?
? choice
* What would it cost you to let go?   flavour:listen mem:cost
  them: Nothing, if I'm honest. Only the habit. Habits feel like walls, from the inside.
  + them: Nothing at all. Only a habit I've worn so long it feels like a wall. You've asked the right question. I'll think on it.   [nod]
* Tell Bakshi before you dust the model again.   flavour:cheer mem:dust
  them: That's a very short deadline. I dust it a great deal.   [laugh]
* Someone trusted me with something big once. It changed me.   flavour:share mem:trusted
  them: Did it? Then perhaps it's time I was that someone.
them: Gently, now. I'll find Bakshi at the shift change. Before I can straighten anything else.

== beat 5
// DAY: 22
// CRESCENDO rides on: comet viewing (event comet, Day 22), the observatory's back window, 22:10-22:40, after the knot; day: 22; cast: raman, bakshi-sol (the bridge speaker, on the night helm), sato (at the scope); quiet ending: beat 6
// As built (ST1, 2026-10-31): the game's comet puts him at the observatory's back window (cometScope, 21:30-22:40) and
// Bakshi on the night helm (the bridge): Bakshi speaks over the comm, so isn't in the cast. The scope's host is Mori, not
// Sato (Sato hosts the bow lounge glass): her one line here is Mori's, the person at the scope (the lead's choice 3a).
tone: resolution
tier: friend
where: Observatory
when: beat:raman.4 event:comet day:22
crescendo: trad:cometScope
quiet: 6
cast: mori
title: The comet with Dev Raman
hint: The back window. Stand with me? It's nearly time.
mem: whoflew
flag: raman.comet
keepsake: the tiny shuttle he carved, a Tern for your shelf
learn: Dev Raman gave the comet turn to Sol Bakshi, and told his daughter Asel she could learn to fly.
them: The back window. Best seat for it. Bakshi's on the helm. I'm not. I've both hands on the sill instead.   [laugh]
bakshi-sol: Bridge to observatory. Coming round now. Gently.
them: Listen to that. Nervous and correct. That's how you should sound the first time.
mori: Here it comes. Look. Dead across the middle of the glass.   [gesture]
them: Oh, that's lovely. Exactly across. Better than I'd have done. Don't tell anyone I said so.
them: Asel's watching too. From Lisk, this same minute. I asked her to. Ah. My device. That's her.   [listen]
them: She's written: "Saw it. Who flew?"
? choice
* What will you tell her?   flavour:listen mem:tellher
  them: The truth. I'll say it out loud first, with you, so I don't soften it.
  + them: The truth. I'll write it with you here, so my hands don't go and make it gentle. Some things shouldn't be gentle.   [nod]
* Tell her the bowl didn't spill.   flavour:cheer mem:bowl
  them: Not a drop. She'll know exactly what that means.   [laugh]
* Tell her someone new flew it. Let her guess the rest.   flavour:share mem:guess
  them: She's my daughter. She'll guess it in one.
them: There. "Someone new. Like you will." Sent.   [nod]
bakshi-sol: Turn complete. Thank you for this. I'll remember it. All of it.
them: So will I, Bakshi. Every comet.
them: This is for you. The fourth shuttle. The Tern, tiny. The bay's got your docking arm. The Tern belongs with you.
them: You were my last good landing. Don't tell anyone it was bumpy.   [laugh]

== beat 6
// DAY: from 23
// QUIET ENDING for beat 5: plays only if beat 5 was missed (not at the back window, or not ready by Day 16); a morning at his window seat, after Day 16
tone: light
tier: friend
where: Observation room
when: beat:raman.4 time:morning !event:comet !flag:raman.comet day:23-
hint: Good morning. Sit? I've news, and something for you.
mem: quietcomet
keepsake: the tiny shuttle he carved, a Tern for your shelf
learn: Dev Raman let Sol Bakshi fly the comet turn, and told Asel she should learn to fly.
mail: 120 | Asel | Asel's written again. She starts lessons on Lisk next month. She says her first landing will be better than mine. It might. Dev
them: Good morning. Bakshi flew it. The comet turn. Right across the big window, exactly.
them: I stood at the back of the observatory like a passenger, hands in my pockets. It was wonderful.   [laugh]
them: Asel watched from Lisk. She wrote to ask who'd flown it. I told her: someone new, the way she will one day.
? choice
* That's a big thing to write. How do you feel?   flavour:listen mem:howfeel
  them: Lighter. As if I'd been carrying a full bowl for years and someone took the other side.
  + them: Lighter. As if I'd carried a full bowl for eleven years and someone finally took the other side. Thank you for asking.   [nod]
* I'm sorry I missed it. Did Bakshi wobble?   flavour:cheer mem:wobble
  them: Not once. I wobbled. Nobody saw.   [laugh]
* My family would want to hear about a night like that.   flavour:share mem:familyhear
  them: Then tell them. Tell them the Wren has a new pilot for comets.
them: And this is yours. The fourth shuttle, the Tern, tiny. You were my last good landing, the night you came. It bumped. Keep that to yourself.
